-0.022125244140625, 0.03466796875, -0.022918701171875, -0.00501251220703125, -0.044281005859375, -0.032501220703125, -0.006404876708984375, -0.038299560546875, 0.03680419921875, 0.0269622802734375, 0.020965576171875, 0.012664794921875, 0.018646240234375, 0.0028553009033203125, -0.0007538795471191406, -0.047637939453125, 0.01453399658203125, -0.010406494140625, -2.0682811737060547e-05, 0.0310516357421875, -0.0256500244140625, 0.005817413330078125, -0.006633758544921875, -0.01210784912109375, 0.001728057861328125, 0.009002685546875, 0.00765228271484375, 0.021331787109375, -0.001705169677734375, 0.00595855712890625, -0.007633209228515625, 0.0210418701171875, -0.0199127197265625, 0.0237884521484375, -0.00084686279296875, 0.0132293701171875, 0.01380157470703125, -0.0269622802734375, -0.0024318695068359375, -0.009002685546875, -0.0289459228515625, -0.0200653076171875, 0.001857757568359375, 0.0197601318359375, -0.042022705078125, 0.0212860107421875, 0.0009036064147949219, 0.00403594970703125, 0.003643035888671875, 0.0037441253662109375, -0.01482391357421875, 0.022308349609375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Level-k models are widely used to describe boundedly rational strategic reasoning in games, where players are categorized by their depth of reasoning. In these models, level-0 players are assumed to choose actions without strategic consideration\u2014often based on salient or default choices\u2014while higher-level players best respond to beliefs about lower-level behavior. Understanding both how level-0 players actually behave and how they are perceived by higher-level players is central to evaluating these models.\n\nLevel-k models have been applied to a variety of strategic settings to explain deviations from Nash equilibrium predictions. The standard approach assumes that level-0 players act non-strategically, for example by choosing randomly or selecting focal points, and that higher-level players iteratively best respond to these assumed behaviors. However, the accuracy of these assumptions about level-0 behavior and beliefs is critical for the predictive power of the models.\n\nTo address these questions, we use the intra-team communication method developed by Burchardi and Penczynski (2014). In this method, teams of participants (typically groups of three to five individuals, recruited from a university subject pool) are asked to discuss and agree on both actions and beliefs in a controlled laboratory setting. Specifically, teams are instructed to either play as a level-0 player\u2014choosing as a non-strategic agent would\u2014or to state what they believe a level-0 player would do. This dual elicitation allows researchers to directly compare observed level-0 actions (actual choices made under non-strategic instructions) with elicited level-0 beliefs (what participants think a non-strategic player would do). The method thus effectively disentangles actual behavior from beliefs about behavior, which is crucial for testing the assumptions of level-k models. Data were analyzed by comparing the distributions of observed and elicited level-0 choices across games.\n\nWe apply this method to two games: the canonical beauty contest game and a variant of the 11-20 game introduced by Goeree et al. (2018). In the beauty contest game, players choose numbers between 0 and 100, aiming to pick a number closest to two-thirds of the average of all chosen numbers. The standard level-k model assumes level-0 players choose randomly or select salient numbers like 50, and higher-level players best respond accordingly. Consistent with Burchardi and Penczynski (2014), we find that the numbers teams identify as level-0 choices (elicited level-0 beliefs) closely match the numbers actually chosen when instructed to play as level-0 (observed level-0 actions). This suggests that, in the beauty contest game, participants\u2019 beliefs about non-strategic behavior are well aligned with actual non-strategic choices.\n\nIn contrast, in the variant of the 11-20 game\u2014where players choose an integer between 11 and 20, with the lowest unique number winning a higher prize\u2014the standard model assumes level-0 players choose randomly or select the lowest number. However, our results show that the numbers teams believe a level-0 player would choose (elicited level-0 beliefs) do not align with the numbers actually chosen when instructed to play as level-0 (observed level-0 actions), and both differ from the standard level-0 assumption. This divergence, which echoes the behavioral pattern documented by Goeree et al. (2018), suggests that the mismatch between beliefs and actions at the level-0 stage may contribute to deviations from standard level-k predictions.\n\nOur findings contribute to the literature by clarifying when and how the standard level-0 assumptions hold, and by highlighting the importance of distinguishing between actual non-strategic behavior and beliefs about such behavior. In the beauty contest game, the alignment between beliefs and actions supports the standard modeling approach, while in the 11-20 game, the divergence calls for a more nuanced specification of level-0 behavior. This comparative analysis underscores the need to refine both the specification of level-0 behavior and the elicitation of beliefs to improve the predictive power of level-k models.\n\nIn summary, our study demonstrates that the validity of level-0 assumptions varies across games, and that both actual non-strategic behavior and beliefs about such behavior must be carefully considered. Future research should further investigate the determinants of level-0 choices and beliefs in a broader range of strategic environments, and explore how these insights can inform the development of more accurate behavioral game-theoretic models."}}